{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Z1cN3tKpZN3PXeGDyNzJ5","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile a comprehensive report.\n\n## WHIR Opening Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase, here's a comprehensive understanding of the WHIR opening code path and its performance characteristics:\n\n### **1. WHIR Code Flow (GPU Implementation)**\n\n**Entry point:** `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:62-531`\n\n**Main phases:**\n\n1. **Proof-of-Work Grinding** (line 91-92)\n   - `grind_gpu(mu_pow_bits)` before μ batching\n\n2. **Algebraic Batching** (line 94-137)\n   - Sample μ randomness\n   - Call `whir_algebraic_batch_traces()` kernel to batch all committed matrices into single f polynomial\n   - Batching happens **across all AIRs at once** using `BatchingTracePacket` array (one per matrix)\n\n3. **MLE Coefficient Transform** (line 139-166)\n   - `batch_ntt_small()` - small NTT on l_skip dimension\n   - `transpose_fp_to_fpext_vec()` - convert base field to extension field\n   - `mle_interpolate_stage_ext()` - apply subset-zeta transform (l_skip stages)\n   - Result: `f_coeffs` in MLE coefficient form of size `2^m` EF elements\n\n4. **WHIR Rounds Loop** (line 199-515)\n   - **Outer loop:** per WHIR round (typically 2-4 rounds for m=20-24)\n   - **Inner loop:** k_whir sumcheck rounds per WHIR round (k_whir=4 typically means 4x folding per round)\n\n### **2. Per-Round Operations (Inside the Round Loop)**\n\nFor each of `k_whir` sumcheck iterations within a WHIR round:\n\n1. **Sumcheck Round** (line 224-241)\n   - GPU kernel: `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()`\n   - Computes s(X) = sum_y f(X,y) * w(X,y) for X ∈ {1,2}\n   - Uses coefficient form for f and moment form for w\n   - **Cost:** O(2^(m-round)) with block reduction\n   - Output: 2 EF values (s[0], s[1])\n\n2. **Proof-of-Work Grinding** (line 243-247)\n   - `grind_gpu(folding_pow_bits)` per sumcheck round\n\n3. **Folding** (line 254-270)\n   - GPU kernel: `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments()`\n   - Folds both f and w: \n     - `f_folded[y] = f[2y] + alpha * f[2y+1]`\n     - `w_folded[y] = (1-alpha) * w[2y] + (2*alpha - 1) * w[2y+1]`\n   - Reduces size by 2x\n   - **Cost:** O(2^(m-round))\n\n4. **After k_whir Folding Steps** (line 272-354):\n   - Convert folded f coefficients to base field: `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix()`\n   - **Polynomial Commitment** (non-final rounds only):\n     - `batch_expand_pad()` - pad from `2^(m-k_whir)` to RS domain size\n     - `batch_ntt()` - NTT to evaluation domain (D_EF parallel NTTs for quaternion field)\n     - Build Merkle tree of RS codewords\n     - Observe root commitment\n\n5. **Query Phase** (line 356-464):\n   - Sample `num_queries` random query indices\n   - **Initial round only** (line 375-442):\n     - `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on original RS-codeword trees for all AIRs\n     - `batch_open_rows()` for merkle proof paths\n   - **Later rounds** (line 444-463):\n     - `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on g_tree\n     - `batch_open_rows()` on g_tree\n\n6. **Weight Moment Update** (line 472-510):\n   - Non-final rounds only\n   - GPU kernel: `w_moments_accumulate()`\n   - Updates w_moments: `w(T) += gamma * eq(z0, T) + gamma^i * eq(z_i, T)`\n   - Computes using powers-of-2 trick for efficiency\n   - **Cost:** O(height * num_queries * log_height)\n\n### **3. GPU Kernels Involved**\n\n| Kernel | Location | Cost | Purpose |\n|--------|----------|------|---------|\n| `whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel` | whir.cu:24-74 | O(stacked_height × num_packets) | Batch trace matrices with mu powers |\n| `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round_kernel` | whir.cu:83-136 | O(2^(m-round)) with block reduction | Compute sumcheck polynomial values |\n| `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments_kernel` | whir.cu:141-166 | O(2^(m-round)) | Fold coefficients & moments |\n| `w_moments_accumulate_kernel` | whir.cu:196-221 | O(height × num_queries × log_height) | Update moment vector with gamma terms |\n| `batch_ntt_small()` | batch_ntt_small.cu | O(height * log(l_skip)) | Small radix-2 NTT on l_skip bits |\n| `batch_ntt()` | ntt.cu | O(height * log(height)) | Full NTT to RS domain |\n| `batch_expand_pad()` | matrix.cu | O(height × D_EF) | Zero-pad and reorder for NTT |\n| Merkle operations | merkle_tree.cu | O(num_queries × log(domain_size)) | Tree construction & proofs |\n\n### **4. Multi-AIR Scaling Characteristics**\n\n**Key insight:** WHIR uses **algebraic batching across AIRs**, not sequential processing.\n\n- **Algebraic batching phase** (line 102-137):\n  ```\n  for each AIR in stacked_per_commit:\n    create BatchingTracePacket with (ptr, height, width, row_start, mu_idx)\n  call whir_algebraic_batch_traces(packets)  // ALL AIRs batched in one kernel call\n  ```\n  - **Per-AIR cost:** O(trace_width × height)\n  - **Batched cost:** O(sum(widths) × height) in **single kernel call**\n  - No sequential per-AIR overhead after initial batching\n\n- **Merkle operations** (initial round only):\n  ```\n  get trees from all AIRs\n  call batch_query_merkle_proofs(trees)  // Batch across AIRs\n  call batch_open_rows(backing_matrices)  // Batch across AIRs\n  ```\n  - **Per-AIR cost:** O(num_queries × tree_depth)\n  - **Batched across AIRs** - see lines 400-425\n\n- **WHIR rounds themselves** (lines 199-515):\n  - **Single batched f polynomial** for all AIRs combined\n  - **No per-AIR iterations** within sumcheck/folding loops\n  - Subsequent rounds operate on **accumulated polynomial**, not individual matrices\n\n**Scaling factor:** With N AIRs of width w_i:\n- Total stacked width = Σ(w_i)\n- Cost grows **linearly** with Σ(w_i), not with N (after algebraic batching)\n\n### **5. Batching & Optimization Opportunities**\n\n**Current batching:**\n1. ✓ **Algebraic batching** - all AIRs combined before WHIR (single mu polynomial)\n2. ✓ **Merkle batching** - `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` across multiple trees\n3. ✓ **NTT batching** - `batch_ntt()` processes D_EF parallel polynomials\n\n**Potential overlapping/opportunities:**\n1. **Async Merkle operations** (lines 400-425):\n   - Merkle proof computation could overlap with NTT of g_coeffs\n   - Currently sequential: NTT → Merkle tree build → queries\n\n2. **W_moments accumulation pipelining** (lines 472-510):\n   - Powers-of-2 computation is CPU-side (lines 482-496)\n   - Could be moved to GPU or overlapped with other operations\n\n3. **Per-round work overlap**:\n   - Each WHIR round does: NTT → Merkle build → queries\n   - Could start query phase while building tree for next iteration\n\n4. **Memory reuse**:\n   - After round 0, original `stacked_per_commit` is dropped (line 442)\n   - Single polynomial streams through subsequent rounds (minimal memory footprint)\n\n### **6. WHIR Round Scaling with Number of Variables (m)**\n\nNumber of WHIR rounds: `ceil((m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir)`\n- Example: m=20, log_final_poly_len=1, k_whir=4 → (20-1)/4 = ~5 rounds\n\nPer-round cost breakdown:\n```\nk_whir sumcheck rounds:\n  - k_whir × [sumcheck(O(2^(m-k*round))) + fold(O(2^(m-k*round))) + grind]\nPolynomial operations (non-final rounds):\n  - split_ext_to_base: O(2^(m-k_whir) × D_EF)\n  - batch_expand_pad: O(2^log_rs_domain_size × D_EF)\n  - batch_ntt: O(2^log_rs_domain_size × log(log_rs_domain_size))\n  - merkle_tree_build: O(2^log_rs_domain_size)\nQuery phase:\n  - w_moments_accumulate: O(2^(m-k_whir) × num_queries × log(m-k_whir))\n  - merkle_proofs: O(num_queries × log_rs_domain_size)\n```\n\n### **File Locations Summary**\n\n| Component | File |\n|-----------|------|\n| GPU WHIR prover | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` |\n| WHIR kernels | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` |\n| CPU reference | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/whir.rs` |\n| Config/params | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/config.rs` |\n| Merkle operations | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` |\n| NTT operations | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/ntt.rs` |\n\nThe 125ms at APC 300 (11% of STARK time) reflects the cost of running multiple WHIR rounds with multiple sumcheck iterations, Merkle commitments per round, and query phase processing - all scaled across the polynomial size but with good batching across multiple AIRs."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd67acf5-7001-4f85-a6f5-23b121ad763a","uuid":"72d25a34-7400-4646-911c-312b385322ac","timestamp":"2026-04-15T08:51:42.018Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm optimizing a GPU-accelerated STARK prover. WHIR is a polynomial commitment opening scheme that takes 125ms at APC 300 (11% of STARK excl trace time).\n\nThoroughness: medium\n\nIn /home/georg/stark-backend, I need to understand:\n\n1. The WHIR opening code path. Look in `crates/cuda-backend/` for anything related to WHIR, `prove_whir_opening`, or similar.\n\n2. How many rounds of WHIR opening are there? What happens in each round?\n\n3. What GPU kernels are involved: `whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel`, NTTs, Merkle tree operations, `grind_kernel`.\n\n4. How does the cost scale with the number of AIRs? Are there per-AIR operations in WHIR or is it batched?\n\n5. Any opportunity for overlapping computation across WHIR rounds or batching operations.\n\nReport: the WHIR code flow, per-round operations, and scaling characteristics with AIR count.","agentId":"ac6bc0f5b659b3f6f","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile a comprehensive report.\n\n## WHIR Opening Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase, here's a comprehensive understanding of the WHIR opening code path and its performance characteristics:\n\n### **1. WHIR Code Flow (GPU Implementation)**\n\n**Entry point:** `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` in `/home/georg/stark-ba