but it only saved about 5ms and was reverted. So that optimization path is exhausted.\n\nLet me reconsider what else might be contributing to the bottleneck here.\n\nLooking at the WHIR opening timing more carefully: it's 125ms at APC 300 and 219ms at APC 0, which is a 1.75x scaling difference. Even if WHIR could scale 2x better, that would only save about 15ms—not a major win.\n\nBut there's something odd I'm noticing with the MLE Rounds timing. It's actually getting worse at higher APC values: 118ms at APC 0 versus 163ms at APC 300, which is a 1.38x slowdown. That's the opposite of what I'd expect.\n\nBreaking this down by segment, the per-segment MLE time is about 24ms at APC 0 but jumps to 82ms at APC 300—a 3.4x difference. This tracks with the AIR distribution: at APC 300 I have 623 AIRs split across 2 segments (312 per segment), while at APC 0 I have 99 AIRs across 5 segments (20 per segment). So each segment at APC 300 has roughly 16x more AIRs, and with batched kernels this is likely causing the scaling issue.\n\nWhat's interesting is that the total cell count per segment is nearly identical—around 381-406M cells—so the slowdown isn't about raw cell processing. Instead, the MLE kernel has per-trace overhead that scales with AIR count rather than cell count. At APC 300, each AIR only has about 1.3M cells, while at APC 0 each AIR has around 19M cells. This per-trace overhead—descriptor processing, block scheduling, and similar operations—dominates when there are many small traces rather than fewer large ones.\n\nWith the 1-block-per-trace approach, APC 300 launches 312 blocks per round versus just 20 at APC 0. Each block at APC 300 processes roughly 93K cells per round, which fits comfortably in L2 cache at 372KB. However, 312 blocks creates scheduling pressure—about 2.4 waves across 128 SMs—and at roughly 0.2ms per block, that compounds to around 8.4ms per segment across all 14 rounds.\n\nBut the actual kernel time is 82ms, roughly 10x higher than my estimate