R is bottlenecked by sequential fractional sumcheck, Trace Commit is already optimized with scatter kernels, and Openings is constrained by algebraic batching in WHIR. Round 0 seems like the most promising area to revisit since it's the largest remaining component without clear optimization constraints.\n\nLooking deeper at the fractional sumcheck kernels, the compute_round_and_revert kernel dominates at 70ms across 141 instances, followed by bit reversal and multifold operations—these sequential dependencies make it hard to parallelize further, but the precompute and tree-building kernels might have room for kernel fusion or better memory access patterns. Total fractional sumcheck GPU: ~233ms\n\nThis is sequential within each segment's GKR proof. With 2 segments, that's ~116ms per segment.\n\nThe GKR input eval is multi-streamed (8 threads) and takes 369ms total. Input eval GPU time is ~502ms. So:\n- Input eval wall time: ~200ms (502ms GPU / 2.5x multi-stream utilization)  \n- Fractional sumcheck wall time: ~169ms (remaining from 369ms total)\n\nSo fractional sumcheck is about 169ms = 46% of LogUp GKR time. This is a sequential phase that's hard to parallelize.\n\nWhat if we could speed up the fractional sumcheck kernels?\n\nLooking at the performance breakdown, most of these kernels are called repeatedly but take only microseconds per invocation. The `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` runs 141 times at 0.5ms each, while `multifold_kernel` and `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel` each run 14 times at 3-3.4ms per call. The `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel` is called 6 times at 8.3ms per call. All of these are fundamentally sequential and kernel-execution-bound, so the only real optimization path is making the kernels themselves more efficient. There's also a `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` that's called 1370 times but only takes 6.5 microseconds per call. So if I'm looking at the structure of these kernel calls — roughly 685 per segment across multiple GKR layers and fractional sumcheck rounds — batching them could save significant overhead. If each round has around 49 calls that could be combined into one launch, that's nearly a quarter millisecond saved per round, which compounds to roughly 34ms across all layers and rounds. Let me verify the math on how many segments and rounds we're actually dealing with here.\n\nThe kernel overhead is eating up time comparable to the actual computation, so batching would help, but we're still only looking at 5-7ms of savings, which isn't quite enough to hit our target.\n\nI'm wondering if there's a different angle — what if I could pipeline the work across segments? While one segment is running its Openings phase on the GPU, the next segment's trace data could be getting prepared on the CPU in parallel. The Openings phase takes about 201ms at APC 300, so there's real opportunity there if the trace commit for the following segment doesn't have hard dependencies on the current one finishing.\n\nLooking at STARK v2, each segment is actually an independent proof that gets aggregated later through recursive composition, so there's no reason they have to be strictly sequential. The prover code processes them in a loop right now, but if I could overlap Segment 0's Openings with Segment 1's Trace Commit, that could save meaningful time.\n\nLet me work through the math: if I pipeline these operations so Segment 1's Trace Commit runs in parallel with Segment 0's Openings phase, the critical path becomes 556ms for Segment 0 plus 555ms for Segment 1 minus the 99ms overlap, bringing the total down to around 1012ms. That's roughly 102ms of savings, which would improve the APC 300 to APC 0 ratio from about 1.78 down to 1.76.\n\nBut I need to think about whether this is actually feasible. Both the Openings phase and Trace Commit phase use the GPU—Openings for WHIR operations and Trace Commit for NTT and Merkle tree work. At APC 300, peak GPU memory during proof is already 1.6GB, so running both phases