{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LBPnJbQUF1PtjTWU4E8f5J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WjsSXXsH1KDYf4sh6MuT1p","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-1130-batch-small-air-round0-descriptors\n\nBatch Round 0 zerocheck and logup evaluation for small AIRs using descriptor-array CUDA kernels with caller-level routing. At APC 300, Round 0 takes 180ms and shows zero scaling (177ms at APC 0 vs 180ms at APC 300 despite 2.35x fewer cells) because per-AIR overhead (kernel launches, buffer allocation, CPU work) scales with the 6.3x increase in AIR count. By batching small AIRs (intermediates < 512KB) into single descriptor-array kernel launches while routing large AIRs to the existing multi-stream path, we eliminate ~500 kernel launches per segment and allow the multi-stream threads to focus on the few large GPU-bound AIRs. Previous attempts (2026-04-13-1600, 2026-04-13-1700) proved the approach is viable — the CUDA kernels and caller-level routing worked, but testing was blocked by an unrelated GKR OOM that has since been resolved.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2443,"cache_read_input_tokens":174581,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2443},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd67acf5-7001-4f85-a6f5-23b121ad763a","uuid":"6335055c-c0cf-4b7c-ac1d-ae6c921ffe67"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjsSXXsH1KDYf4sh6MuT1p","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd67acf5-7001-4f85-a6f5-23b121ad763a","uuid":"c3e7b67d-47a0-4bb3-a139-d4f743c92137","timestamp":"2026-04-15T09:16:25.938Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-1130-batch-small-air-round0-descriptors\n\nBatch Round 0 zerocheck and logup evaluation for small AIRs using descriptor-array CUDA kernels with caller-level routing. At APC 300, Round 0 takes 180ms and shows zero scaling (177ms at APC 0 vs 180ms at APC 300 despite 2.35x fewer cells) because per-AIR overhead (kernel launches, buffer allocation, CPU work) scales with the 6.3x increase in AIR count. By batching small AIRs (intermediates < 512KB) into single descriptor-array kernel launches while routing large AIRs to the existing multi-stream path, we eliminate ~500 kernel launches per segment and allow the multi-stream threads to focus on the few large GPU-bound AIRs. Previous attempts (2026-04-13-1600, 2026-04-13-1700) proved the approach is viable — the CUDA kernels and caller-level routing worked, but testing was blocked by an unrelated GKR OOM that has since been resolved.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold","+# 2026-04-15-1130-batch-small-air-round0-descriptors"," ","-Decouple the VPMM allocation routing threshold from the physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter. Currently, allocations smaller than `page_size` (16 MiB) use `cudaMallocAsync` while larger ones go through the VPMM page-management system. The previous page-size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB gave a surprising −186 ms on APC 300 STARK excl trace, with ~136 ms coming from medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) bypassing VPMM overhead. By raising the routing threshold to 64 MiB (4× page_size) without changing the page size itself, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range (including per-thread GKR intermediates at ~20–40 MiB each) will also bypass VPMM, avoiding per-allocation cuMemCreate, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead while preserving the 16 MiB page granularity for truly large allocations (>64 MiB).","+Batch Round 0 zerocheck and logup evaluation for small AIRs using descriptor-array CUDA kernels with caller-level routing. At APC 300, Round 0 takes 180ms and shows zero scaling (177ms at APC 0 vs 180ms at APC 300 despite 2.35x fewer cells) because per-AIR overhead (kernel launches, buffer allocation, CPU work) scales with the 6.3x increase in AIR count. By batching small AIRs (intermediates < 512KB) into single descriptor-array kernel launches while routing large AIRs to the existing multi-stream path, we eliminate ~500 kernel launches per segment and allow the multi-stream threads to focus on the few large GPU-bound AIRs. Previous attempts (2026-04-13-1600, 2026-04-13-1700) proved the approach is viable — the CUDA kernels and caller-level routing worked, but testing was blocked by an unrelated GKR OOM that has since been resolved."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold\n\nDecouple the VPMM allocation routing threshold from the physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter. Currently, allocations smaller than `page_size` (16 MiB) use `cudaMallocAsync` while larger ones go through the VPMM page-management system. The previous page-size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB gave a surprising −186 ms on APC 300 STARK excl trace, with ~136 ms coming from medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) bypassing VPMM overhead. By raising the routing threshold to 64 MiB (4× page_size) without changing the page size itself, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range (including per-thread GKR intermediates at ~20–40 MiB each) will also bypass VPMM, avoiding per-allocation cuMemCreate, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead while preserving the 16 MiB page granularity for truly large allocations (>64 MiB).\n"}}
