{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUc9b84dvm4rx23DVTWfNN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch Small AIR Round 0 Descriptors\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce Round 0 wall time at APC 300 from ~180ms to ~80-130ms by batching small AIRs into descriptor-array CUDA kernel launches, eliminating per-AIR kernel launch overhead and CPU work for the majority of AIRs. This targets the worst-scaling component of STARK excl trace (0.98x scaling from APC 0 to APC 300).\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\tRound 0 is orchestrated by `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (lines 1022-1447).\n10\t\n11\t### Phase 1: Work item preparation (lines 1022-1152)\n12\tFor each present AIR, build a `Round0AirWorkItem` with:\n13\t- Pre-computed buffer sizes via `_zerocheck_r0_intermediates_buffer_size()` (line 1045) and `_logup_r0_intermediates_buffer_size()` (line 1067)\n14\t- Extract transform tables per unique constraint degree\n15\t- Batch array offsets for output polynomials\n16\t\n17\t### Phase 2: Multi-stream dispatch (lines 1204-1420)\n18\tWhen AIR count >= 100 (APC 300 has ~312 per segment):\n19\t- Pre-allocate per-thread `Round0ThreadBuffers` at 95th percentile sizes (lines 1229-1273)\n20\t- Round-robin work distribution across 8 threads (line 1330)\n21\t- Background precompute thread for logup combinations (lines 1341-1382)\n22\t- Each worker thread calls `process_air_round0()` per AIR sequentially (lines 1385-1406)\n23\t\n24\t### Per-AIR processing in `process_air_round0` (lines 244-389)\n25\t1. Zerocheck evaluation: launch `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel` (line 291)\n26\t2. Zerocheck extraction: launch `_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly` GPU kernel (line 314)\n27\t3. Logup evaluation: build interaction DAG on CPU (lines 193-239), launch `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel` (line 346)\n28\t4. Logup extraction: launch `_round0_extract_logup_polys` GPU kernel (line 373)\n29\t\n30\t### Why Round 0 doesn't scale\n31\tAt APC 300: 623 AIRs (312 per segment), 8 threads, ~78 AIRs per thread.\n32\tPer-AIR overhead: 2-4 kernel launches, CPU DAG building, buffer checks, H2D copies.\n33\tGPU kernel time: ~640ms total → 80ms ideal wall time with 8 threads.\n34\tActual wall time: 180ms → 100ms of non-kernel overhead across all threads.\n35\tThe overhead scales with AIR count (6.3x more at APC 300), offsetting the 2.35x reduction in per-AIR compute.\n36\t\n37\t## Changes\n38\t\n39\t### Change 1: CUDA batched Round 0 kernels\n40\t\n41\t**Files**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`, `logup_round0.cu`\n42\t\n43\tAdd batched descriptor-array kernel variants. Reference reverted commits `0c699f47` and `bcb1c0d2` for the kernel design:\n44\t\n45\t**Zerocheck descriptor struct** (`Round0ZcCtx`):\n46\t```c\n47\tstruct Round0ZcCtx {\n48\t    const Fp *selectors_cube;\n49\t    const Fp *preprocessed;\n50\t    const Fp *const *main_parts;\n51\t    const FpExt *eq_cube;\n52\t    const FpExt *d_lambda_pows;\n53\t    const Fp *public_values;\n54\t    const Rule *d_rules;\n55\t    size_t rules_len;\n56\t    const size_t *d_used_nodes;\n57\t    size_t used_nodes_len;\n58\t    size_t lambda_len;\n59\t    uint32_t buffer_size;\n60\t    Fp *d_intermediates;     // Points into shared intermediates arena\n61\t    uint32_t skip_domain;\n62\t    uint32_t num_x;\n63\t    uint32_t height;\n64\t    Fp g_shift;\n65\t};\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\t**Logup descriptor struct** (`Round0LogupCtx`): same pattern with logup-specific fields (numer_weights, denom_weights, denom_sum_init).\n69\t\n70\t**Batched kernel**: one CUDA block per AIR. Each block reads its descriptor from the context array and executes the same logic as the per-AIR coset-parallel kernel. Grid: `dim3(num_airs, num_cosets)`. Uses the same `NttEvalContext` template internally.\n71\t\n72\t**Batched reduction**: after the main kernel, launch `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` (already exists in `sumcheck.cuh`) to reduce per-block partial sums to per-AIR outputs.\n73\t\n74\t**Batched extraction**: a new `batched_round0_extract_kernel` that processes all batched AIRs' extraction in one launch. Each block applies its AIR's transform matrix to convert evaluation sums to polynomial coefficients.\n75\t\n76\t**Why this helps**: eliminates ~500 kernel launches per segment (250 zerocheck + 250 logup → 2 batched launches per coset group). L2 cache is preserved because small AIR intermediates are <10KB each; 128 concurrent blocks × 10KB = 1.28MB, well within the 72MB L2.\n77\t\n78\t### Change 2: Rust FFI bindings\n79\t\n80\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n81\t\n82\tAdd `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs matching the CUDA descriptors: `Round0ZcCtx`, `Round0LogupCtx`, `Round0BlockCtx`. Add safe wrapper functions for the batched kernel launchers and the batched extraction kernel.\n83\t\n84\t### Change 3: Batched orchestration module\n85\t\n86\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs` (new file)\n87\t\n88\tFunctions:\n89\t1. `identify_batchable_airs(work_items, max_intermediates) -> Vec<bool>`: Mark AIRs as batchable if BOTH their zerocheck and logup intermediates sizes are below `max_intermediates` (512KB, chosen so that 128 concurrent blocks stay within L2 cache). Non-batchable criteria: intermediates > 512KB per AIR, or AIR has no zerocheck constraints.\n90\t\n91\t2. `batch_round0_small_airs(batchable_items, d_batch_ptr, tables) -> Result<()>`:\n92\t   - Group batchable AIRs by `num_cosets` (constraint_degree determines coset count; same coset count required for grid.y dimension)\n93\t   - For each coset group:\n94\t     a. Allocate shared intermediates arena: `sum(per_air_intermediates_size)` in one `DeviceBuffer`\n95\t     b. Build `Round0ZcCtx` descriptors on host with per-AIR intermediates pointers into the arena\n96\t     c. Upload descriptor arrays (contexts + block mappings) via single H2D copy\n97\t     d. Launch batched zerocheck kernel\n98\t     e. Launch batched zerocheck extraction kernel\n99\t     f. Build `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors (reuse intermediates arena)\n100\t     g. Upload logup descriptors\n101\t     h. Launch batched logup kernel\n102\t     i. Launch batched logup extraction kernel\n103\t   - The logup evaluation still needs per-AIR interaction DAG rules on device. Pre-compute ALL batchable AIRs' DAGs in a single CPU loop before the kernel launch, uploading all rules as a concatenated device buffer with per-AIR offset pointers.\n104\t\n105\t3. `merge_skip_mask(batchable, work_items) -> Vec<bool>`: Create skip mask for existing multi-stream path so it only processes non-batched (large) AIRs.\n106\t\n107\tAll functions marked `#[inline(never)]` to prevent Rust optimizer interference with the existing `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` function.\n108\t\n109\t### Change 4: Caller-level routing\n110\t\n111\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n112\t\n113\tModify the caller of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` (in `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` or equivalent) to:\n114\t1. Before calling `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`, call `identify_batchable_airs()` on the work items\n115\t2. If any AIRs are batchable, call `batch_round0_small_airs()` for the batched subset\n116\t3. Pass a `skip_traces: &[bool]` parameter to `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` so it skips already-processed AIRs\n117\t4. After both paths complete, polynomial results are already in the shared `d_batch_array`\n118\t\n119\tMinimal changes to `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`: add a single `.filter()` on the work items iterator using the skip mask (lines ~1110, ~1330). This is the same approach that succeeded in the `batch-round0-caller-routing` task.\n120\t\n121\t### Change 5: Remove unused CUDA template instantiations\n122\t\n123\t**Files**: `zerocheck_round0.cu`, `logup_round0.cu`\n124\t\n125\tThe previous OOM was caused by additional CUDA template instantiations consuming GPU memory. Audit the existing template instantiations for `NEEDS_SHMEM=true` variants. Since the benchmark uses `l_skip=4` (skip_domain=16), and `NEEDS_SHMEM=true` is only needed when `buffer_size * num_cosets > shared_memory_limit`, remove or guard unused instantiations behind a compile-time flag.\n126\t\n127\t## Invariants\n128\t\n129\t1. **Correctness**: Batched path must produce identical polynomial coefficients as the per-AIR path. Verify by running both paths and comparing the `d_batch_array` output.\n130\t2. **No APC 0 regression**: APC 0 has ~20 AIRs per segment (below the 100-AIR multi-threading threshold). The batched path should be skipped entirely at APC 0.\n131\t3. **Memory safety**: The shared intermediates arena must have correct per-AIR offsets. Each AIR's intermediates pointer must not overlap with another AIR's.\n132\t4. **Coset grouping**: All AIRs in a batched kernel launch must share the same `num_cosets` value (determines grid.y dimension). Different coset groups get separate launches.\n133\t5. **VPMM pool state**: The batched arena allocation should not change pool state for subsequent phases. Use a single large allocation that is freed before GKR begins.\n134\t\n135\t## Measurement Plan\n136\t\n137\t1. Build: `cargo check -p openvm-cuda-backend` to verify compilation\n138\t2. Test: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` to verify existing tests pass\n139\t3. Correctness check: Add a `#[cfg(debug_assertions)]` comparison that runs both batched and per-AIR paths and asserts polynomial equality at APC 300\n140\t4. Benchmark APC 0: verify no regression (Round 0 should be unchanged, single-threaded path)\n141\t5. Benchmark APC 300: measure STARK excl trace and Round 0 component\n142\t6. Benchmark APC 100: verify improvement scales with AIR count\n143\t7. Profile with nsight at APC 300: confirm reduced kernel launch count and improved GPU utilization in Round 0\n144\t\n145\tExpected results:\n146\t- Round 0 at APC 300: 180ms → 80-130ms (1.4-2.3x improvement)\n147\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1114ms → 1014-1064ms (5-9% improvement)\n148\t- APC 0: no change\n149\t\n150\t## Rollback Criteria\n151\t\n152\tRevert if any of:\n153\t- Round 0 at APC 300 improves by less than 20ms (below noise threshold)\n154\t- APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 20ms\n155\t- Any APC configuration produces incorrect proofs\n156\t- GPU OOM prevents measurement at APC 300 (indicates template instantiation bloat)\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WqBVpv4iTXwmt11WWUnn54","session_id":"cd67acf5-7001-4f85-a6f5-23b121ad763a","uuid":"751292a3-e5b1-4a0e-a2b1-d6de61bf8b9b","timestamp":"2026-04-15T09:18:05.936Z"}
