{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EUxr45cuZ7Yy5QTuC3zZei","type":"tool_result","content":"264\t\n265\t## 2026-04-15-1000-vpmm-bulk-page-creation\n266\t\n267\t**Idea**: Reduce cuMemCreate overhead by increasing the default VPMM page size from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (8x the CUDA minimum granularity).\n268\t\n269\t**Result**: success — STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → 1110ms (2.21x lower vs baseline 2455ms). (vs previous: −186ms, 1.17x lower). No regressions at APC 0.\n270\t\n271\t**Summary**: The original plan was to batch cuMemCreate calls via a single bulk allocation with offset-based cuMemMap. During implementation, discovered that the RTX 4090 (driver 570.195.03) does not support cuMemMap with non-zero offset or partial-size mapping — any cuMemMap call must map the entire physical allocation. Pivoted to increasing the default page size from 2 MiB to 16 MiB, which reduces cuMemCreate calls by 8x and routes medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) through cudaMallocAsync. Trace Commit improved by 50ms (as expected from fewer cuMemCreate calls), and LogUp GKR/Constraints improved by an additional 136–290ms, likely from reduced VPMM management overhead for medium-sized buffers. Cumulative STARK excl trace vs baseline is now 2.21x at APC 300.\n272\t\n273\t## 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold\n274\t\n275\t**Idea**: Decouple VPMM allocation routing threshold from physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter (default 4x page_size = 64 MiB) so 16-64 MiB allocations bypass VPMM.\n276\t\n277\t**Result**: failure — APC 0: STARK excl trace 1779ms → 1830ms (+51ms regression, exceeds 20ms rollback threshold). APC 300: 1110ms → 1130ms (+20ms, no improvement). (vs baseline: 2.17x lower, unchanged from previous)\n278\t\n279\t**Summary**: Introduced configurable `pool_threshold` independent of `page_size` and tested 2x (32 MiB) and 4x (64 MiB) multipliers. Both caused APC 0 regression scaling monotonically with the threshold (2x: +32ms, 4x: +51ms), confirmed by a 1x control run matching the original 1773ms. The regression is distributed across bandwidth-bound phases (LogUp GKR +13ms, MLE Rounds +18ms, Openings +22ms). This is the same VPMM pool state sensitivity seen in 3 prior tasks — routing more allocations through cudaMallocAsync degrades memory access patterns for subsequent kernels. The 16 MiB page size from the previous task appears to be a local optimum.\n280\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WqBVpv4iTXwmt11WWUnn54","session_id":"cd67acf5-7001-4f85-a6f5-23b121ad763a","uuid":"f5befbe5-2c86-43ed-979a-e5442ac1d4d6","timestamp":"2026-04-15T09:22:01.057Z"}
